## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are attracting increasing interests as photocatalysts for solar-driven hydrogen production from water. This paper reports on a comparative study of using either acetic acid (AA) or trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) as the representative UiO-66 organic modulators for synthesizing visible light responsive UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) or \(\mathsf{N O}_{2}\)) photocatalysts for water splitting. The results show that photocatalytic hydrogen generation from a water/methanol mixture can be improved by varying the nature and amount of the modulator employed to prepare the different UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) or \(\mathsf{N O}_{2}\)) solid derivatives. UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) was the most active photocatalyst, followed by UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathsf{N O}_{2}\), both prepared with 12 equivalents of AA with respect to the organic ligand. This UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) solid was more active than the parent MOF in photocatalytic overall water splitting (OWS) (\(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) production of 450 and \(160\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\), respectively, in \(5\;\mathrm{h}\); apparent quantum yield (AQY) at \(400\;\mathsf{n m}\) of \(0.06\%\)) in the absence of methanol and compares favourably with analogous reports. Information on the photocatalytic activity of the most active solids of both series was obtained by means of a series of techniques, including ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) diffuse reflectance, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), laser flash photolysis (LFP), electron spin resonance (ESR), photoluminescence and photoelectrochemical measurements together with density functional theory (DFT) calculations. The results showed that organic acid modulators can be used to enhance the photocatalytic activity of missing linker UiO-66 defective materials in solar-powered water splitting.
## KEYWORDS
heterogeneous photocatalysis, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), UiO-66 materials, carboxylic acid modulators, water splitting, solar light
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordinating polymers (PCPs), are a class of porous crystalline materials made up of multipodal organic ligands coordinated to metals ions, clusters, or chains [1–6]. MOFs are among the most versatile and tuneable materials [7, 8] and can be used in many applications, including gas storage and separation [9, 10], catalysis [11, 12], photocatalysis [13, 14], electrocatalysis [15], biomedicine [16], sensing [17] and many others [18, 19].
In a seminal work, Garcia and co-workers have reported the possibility of using UiO-66(Zr) and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) solids in the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in the presence of methanol as sacrificial electron donor under UV–vis irradiation [20]. Later, in 2017 Huang, Liu and co-workers [21] used a MOF based on \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) ions coordinated with the amino group of MIL-53(Al)-\(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) as photocatalyst for overall water splitting (OWS) into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the absence of sacrificial electron donor. Since then, the number of related studies specially for the HER [13, 22–25]
and to a lesser extent for the more challenging OWS [13] has increased considerably. Some of the strategies reported to enhance MOFs photocatalytic activity for these reactions include [13, 22–26] deposition of metal nanoparticles (NPs) as co-catalysts, encapsulation of organic photosensitizers, preparation of MOFs with specific exposed-facets, development of heterojunctions based on MOF-on-MOF, MOF/inorganic semiconductors, MOF/carbon-based materials, and tuning MOF-exposed facets, among others [13].
Another interesting possibility of improving MOFs photocatalytic activity for hydrogen generation is the development of defective materials [27, 28]. In contrast to these few existing examples, MOF defect engineering has been shown as an appropriate approach to tune several of their physical, chemical, and textural properties [29–31] with applications in different areas, including heterogeneous catalysis [32–37]. In this context, there are several types of defective metal and/or organic ligand in MOFs that can be induced by post-synthetic modification (PSM) or in situ synthesis [29, 32–34, 38, 39]. For example, several studies
have reported the introduction of structural defects in MOFs by PSM methods like thermal, chemical, or plasma treatment [32, 38, 40, 41]. MOFs were reported as photocatalysts for water splitting in 2020, with the possibility of boosting the activity of MIL\(125(\mathrm{Ti}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) by a PSM approach using an oxygen plasma treatment for both photocatalytic HER and OWS reactions [28]. The characterization data showed that this plasma treatment promotes the partial MOF decarboxylation and determines its energy level diagram. The most active defective sample had the most appropriate energy band diagram for both HER and OWS. Another method used to introduce defects in several MOFs, particularly in UiO-66 materials [42–44], is the so-called modulated synthesis [29, 32, 38, 40, 45]. This method consists of preparing the MOFs in the presence of organic (ca formic, acetic, trifluoroacetic, benzoic acids) or inorganic (ca HCl) compounds, sometimes simply referred to as modulators [29]. These modulators compete with the organic ligands during their coordination with the metal nodes, especially in MOFs with a high connectivity of secondary building units (SBUs), i.e., UiO-66 based materials with 12-connected SBU [29, 46]. Some of the structural MOF defects are missing linkers or missing cluster defects [29, 46–48]. In this context, using formic acid as modulator during the preparation of UiO-66(Hf) produced UiO-66 missing-cluster defects in nanoregions with a reo topology [49]. Another study also reported that the modulated synthesis of UiO\(66(\mathrm{Zr})\) using acetic acid (AA) as modulator leads to the formation of missing linker defects [50]. In addition to the type of MOF structural defects [42], the nature and amount of the modulators can also determine their physical, chemical, morphological and textural properties [29]. A comprehensive investigation reported that monocarboxylic acid modulators with a growing acid character, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}<\mathrm{HCOOH}<\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{CHCOOH}<\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{CCOOH},\) gradually increased some textural properties, such as the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area [42]. About 20 equivalents of the modulator had the strongest influence when using organic modulators, while larger amounts had less influence on these properties [42]. In this context, some theoretical [51] and experimental studies have shown that these organic modulators can modify MOF electronic properties such as UiO-66 solids and therefore their photocatalytic activity [27]. As a matter of fact, this former [51] and other theoretical works [36, 52–55] underlined the potential of theory to improve the photoactivity of functionalized UiO-66 defective materials via the setup of accurate structure–property relationships. Nonetheless, a complete theoretical picture addressing both the thermodynamics of the OWS reaction mechanisms, and the photophysics underlying these processes, has not been reported so far. It was also shown that HER photocatalytic activity under UV–vis irradiation can be controlled by Pt NPs supported defective \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) solids prepared with relatively high amounts of AA as modulator (50 to 200 equivalents) with respect to the organic ligand [27]. A volcano relationship was found between the amount of AA and the photocatalytic activity for the series of Pt NPs supported UiO\(66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids. The optimized photocatalyst prepared using 100 equivalents of AA exhibited the highest charge separation efficiency and the fastest relaxation kinetics, as revealed by ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy (TAS). Based on the pertinent results achieved [27] and the reported possibilities to prepare defective MOFs from modulated synthesis [42], it would be of interest to further expand this line of research. For example, to achieve more evidence about the impact of defects generated by AA within \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) during the photocatalytic HER, it is planned to investigate the series of samples in the absence of Pt NPs as co-catalysts. In addition, the possibilities of visible-light responsive and defective MOFs having different energy band level
diagrams like \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NO}_{2}\) with other modulators to boost their photocatalytic activities not only for HER but also for the challenging OWS with high interest for practical applications [13] are also explored.
With these precedents in mind, the present paper describes a comparative study of the performance of \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids prepared using relatively low proportions of AA and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) as modulators during photocatalytic solar-driven overall water splitting reaction. The innovation of this investigation resides in the fact that for the first time we report the development of defective \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) materials from modulated synthesis as an adequate strategy to enhance their photocatalytic activity for water splitting reactions in the absence of any co-catalyst. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first example describing the application of defective MOFs from modulated synthesis as photocatalysts for the OWS. Besides, the photocatalytic activity of the most active sample developed in this study namely \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) from modulated synthesis with AA is higher than that achieved with other MOF-based photocatalysts having in some cases noble metals as co-catalysts (see Section 3.2). In addition, the importance of this study associates with the fundamental understanding of the origin of the activity of the solids under study by experimental characterization using spectroscopic tools like laser flash photolysis (LFP), photoluminescence, electron spin resonance (ESR), electrochemical impedance, and transient photocurrent measurements together with density functional theory (DFT) calculations.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
All the materials employed in this study were supplied by Merck and were of analytical or high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) grade.
## 2.2 Preparation procedures
\(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) materials. The \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) solid was prepared by a solvothermal method as previously reported [42]. Briefly, \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) (0.862 g, 3.699 mmol), 2-aminoterephthalic acid (0.677 g, 3.701 mmol), and Mili-Q \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.200 mL, 11.10 mmol) were mixed with dimethylformamide (DMF; 100 mL, 1291 mmol) in 250 ml volumetric flask. The system was heated at 70 °C and stirred during the preparation to ensure the complete dissolution of the reagents. Once all reagents were dissolved, the stirring magnet was removed from the system and the flask was closed. Then, it was placed in a pre-heated oven at 120 °C for 72 h. The resulting precipitate was recovered by filtration. Then, it was washed 3 times with DMF and three more with methanol. It was further washed in a Soxhlet for 4 h using methanol as the solvent. And finally, the powder was dried under vacuum at 150 °C overnight. Similarly, a series of \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) were prepared in the presence of different amounts of AA or TFA as modulators. The experimental procedure is the same as described for \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) but with the addition of AA 12 equivalents (eq.; 2.542 mL, 36 eq. (7.626 mL), 100 eq. (21.183 mL) or TFA 12 eq. (3.425 mL) or 36 eq. (10.275 mL). Attempts to prepare \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) with 100 eq. of TFA practically did not result in the formation of any solid. The samples were labelled as \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\)-XAA or \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\)-XTFA, where "X" refers to the number of equivalents of modulator employed during the synthesis.
\(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NO}_{2}\) materials. The \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NO}_{2}\) solid was synthesized using a solvothermal method as reported [42]. Briefly, \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) (0.862 g, 3.699 mmol), 2-nitroterpenthalic acid (0.790 g,
3.701 mmol), and Milli-Q \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.200 mL, 11.10 mmol) were mixed with DMF (100 mL, 1291 mmol) in 250 mL volumetric flask. The system was heated at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) while stirring to ensure the complete dissolution of the reagents. Once all reagents were well dissolved, the flask was placed in a pre-heated oven at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) after removing the stirring magnet and closing the system. The resulting precipitate was recovered by filtration. Then, it was washed three times with DMF and subsequently, three times with methanol. It was further washed in a Soxhlet for \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) using methanol as the solvent. Finally, the powder was recovered by drying under vacuum at \(150\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight. The series of modulated UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) samples were similarly prepared with the addition of AA 12 eq (2.542 mL), 36 eq. (7.626 mL), 100 eq. (21.183 mL) or TFA 12 eq. (3.425 mL) or 36 eq. (10.275 mL). Attempts to prepare UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) with 100 eq. of TFA practically did not afford any formation of solid. Samples were labelled as UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-XAA or UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-XTFA where the "X" refers to the number of eq. of modulator employed during the synthesis.
Deposition of Pt NPs within UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA. Pt NPs were deposited within the UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA network using the photodeposition method [56]. Briefly, \(50\,\mathrm{mg}\) of the MOF was suspended in a mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (8 mL) and MeOH (13 mL) using a quartz tube. As platinum precursor, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\cdot(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\), previously dissolved in water (1 mL) was used and added to the solution. Then, the system was sonicated for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) (450 W) to get a good dispersion of the solid. Subsequently, the system was purged with an argon flow for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) to remove the presence of air. The system was maintained under inert atmosphere using a balloon containing Ar. Finally, the system was irradiated with a Xe lamp (150 W) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). The resulting solid was obtained by filtration, washing it several times with Milli-Q water. The sample was placed in an oven at \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) for at least \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). Before use, the solid was activated under vacuum at \(150\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\).
## 2.3 Characterization
## 2.4 Photocatalytic activity
The series of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)) were tested as photocatalysts for water splitting. Briefly, the required amount of photocatalyst (10 mg) was dispersed in a mixture of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (16 mL) and MeOH (4 mL) using a quartz reactor (51 mL). The system was sonicated for 20 min (450 W) to facilitate the solid suspension. In the case of the photocatalytic OWS, the liquid employed for the experiment was Milli-Q water (20 mL). The photoreactor was subsequently purged with an argon flow for 20 min to remove the presence of oxygen. The reaction started by irradiation the top of the reaction by simulated sunlight (LC-8 light guide lamp; 1.5 AM filter) with an irradiance power of \(220\,\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\).
## 3 Results and discussions
## 3.1 Photocatalyst characterization
The series of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids synthesized in the absence or presence of different equivalents of AA or TFA as modulators with respect to the organic ligand were initially characterized by PXRD. Figure 1 shows that all the materials exhibited the expected UiO-66 topology according to the simulated pattern. Previous studies reported that the modulated synthesis of UiO-66 materials by AA can be used to introduce structural defects in the form of ordered missing linkers [41, 50]. In this work, the PXRD of the as-synthesized UiO-66 materials by either AA or TFA did not reveal the presence of diffraction peaks at around \(4^{\circ}\) and \(6^{\circ}\), associated with 100 and 110 reflections of the UiO-66 reo topology, respectively. The absence of these reflections indicates a relatively low proportion or absence of ordered missing cluster defects in the UiO-66 solids [42, 49, 57]. Based on related studies and the present UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids characterization data, the presence of missing linker defects is likely [27, 29, 48, 49, 57–59].
Sample porosity was studied by isothermal \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption measurements (Table 1 and Figs. S4–S7 in the ESM). The modulated synthesis of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids by AA, and especially TFA, increases the porosity (BET and pore volume) in higher extent than the non-modulated materials, which is associated to the partial replacement of terephthalate-based ligands by monocarboxylic acid modulators in the SBU and, as a consequence, higher spaces are generated within the MOF structure [27]. The highest porosities were obtained for UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids when using 12 equivalents of TFA, followed by the samples prepared using the same AA equivalents. These results agree with previous reports that found the porosity of UiO-66(Zr) solid could be increased with TFA
more than by AA [42]. Attempts to use higher equivalents of modulators for TFA (ca 36) or AA (ca 36 or 100) as modulators did not increase the porosity or their photocatalytic activity for HER (Section 3.2). These results are similar to those obtained during UiO-66(Zr) synthesis by either TFA or AA in which the BET surface area rose by up to \(30\%\) using less than 30 equivalents, while higher amounts produced a plateau of surface area values [42].
TGA of \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(a) and Fig. S8 in the ESM) and UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S8 in the ESM) solids were used to estimate the number of structural defects [42]. Based on previous studies, the number of linker defects per \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) formula unit for UiO-66(Zr)-X \(\mathrm{X}\) : \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) can be referred as \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6+x}(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2})_{6-x}\) or \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6+x}(\mathrm{BDC-NO}_{2})_{6-x}\) (1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC)) [42]. Weight losses at between room temperature and about \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were attributable to the removal of water and the AA or TFA modulators. Higher temperatures caused the combustion of the 2-amino or 2-nitroterephthalate ligands and led to a residue above \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) associated with the formation of \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) . For comparison, the TGA curves in Fig. 2 are normalized to the residue obtained after calcination. The results indicate that using increasing amounts of AA from 12 to 100 equivalents as modulator when synthesizing \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NO}_{2}\) solids reduces the proportion of 2-amino or 2- nitroterephthalate organic ligand below the metal content, respectively, as the number of defects increases with the amount of modulator used to synthesize the UiO-66 solids (Fig. 2 and Fig. S8 in the ESM), as other authors have found [42]. In other words, increasing the amount of AA in the synthesis produces a higher proportion of coordinated acetates than the 2-amino or 2- nitroterephthalates in the MOF network. In fact, liquid proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H-NMR) analysis of previously digested UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids prepared using AA show the presence of a singlet signal at \(1.83\;\mathrm{ppm}\) associated with the \(\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\) group of the modulator (Fig. S9 in the ESM).
Additional characterization of the samples UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) by solid-state \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) -NMR revealed the appearance of broad signals associated due to the protons from the organic ligands together with \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -OH signals that overlap with methyl groups of acetates (Fig. S11 in the ESM) [60]. Similarly, the UiO-66 samples prepared with TFA as modulator are characterized by a \(^{19}\mathrm{F}\) -NMR signal at \(-75.69\mathrm{~ppm}\) (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Solid-state \(^{19}\mathrm{F}\) -NMR characterization further confirms the more intense \(^{19}\mathrm{F}\) -NMR signal in the MOF structures prepared with 36 eq. of TFA as modulator (Fig. S12 in the ESM) [54]. Figure 2 also shows that 12 equivalents of TFA produced more defective MOFs than even 100 AA equivalents. The \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) samples prepared with AA or TFA as modulators were characterized by SEM-EDX analysis (Figs. S13–S24 in the ESM) and revealed the presence of the expected elements (C, O, N, Zr, and F) in each case within the MOF particles.
In TGA analysis, the thermal stability of modulated UiO-66(Zr)- X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) generally decreases as the number of defects increases. These observations can be understood by considering the replacement of bipodal terephthalate to bridge the SBU of the MOF compared with the presence of monocarboxylate ligands in these SBU. Regardless of these comments, it is pertinent to mention that to obtain some insights about the nature of structural defects on MOFs advanced spectroscopic techniques such as positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS), ESR spectroscopy, X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES), or extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) among other possible can be used [34, 35, 61, 62].
The particle size distribution and morphology of the series of UiO-66 solids were studied by SEM (Table 1, Fig. 3, and Figs. S25–S29 in the ESM). Non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. 3(a) and Fig. S25 in the ESM) and UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Figs. S27(a) and S28(a) in the ESM) samples were characterized by the presence of intergrown irregularly shaped particles with an estimated average particle size and standard deviations of about \(278\pm108\) and \(311\pm\)
\(248\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. Using 12 equivalents of AA or TFA, the modulated synthesis of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) reduced the particle size distribution with respect to the parent MOFs (Table 1 and Fig. 3 and Figs. S25 and S26 in the ESM). Increasing the amount of AA from 36 or 100 equivalents during the preparation of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) gradually increased the particle size distribution. In contrast to the non-modulated or modulated samples prepared with 36 equivalents of modulator or lower, the UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) samples prepared with 100 equivalents of AA have a well-defined octahedral shape.
The most photocatalytically active UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) samples prepared with 12 equivalents of AA and their parent MOFs were further characterized by XPS (Fig. 4). The deconvolution of the individual elements present in the MOFs can be seen in Figs. S30–S33 in the ESM. The XPS survey analysis of these samples showed the expected C 1s, O 1s, N 1s, and Zr 3p (Fig. 4(a)). High resolution XPS was performed on the different elements. The expected general features of these MOFs are generally described as follows: C 1s spectra (Fig. 4(b)) show the presence of three deconvoluted bands centered at 284.4, 286.5, and 288.5 due to the presence of aromatic C–C \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) atoms, C–N bonds, and \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)groups, respectively. The broad band of O 1s spectra is associated with the presence of oxygen atoms in the MOF as Zr–O bonds (529 eV) and \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\) (531 eV) (Fig. 4(c)). The N 1s spectra of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) are dominated by main bands at 399 and 405 eV due to the presence of amino and nitro groups, respectively (Fig. 4(c)). Zr 2p shows the expected bands for Zr(IV) ions at 182 and 185 eV due to the presence of Zr \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and Zr \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\), respectively (Fig. 4(c)). C 1s XPS comparative analysis (Fig. 4(b)) also reveals a small but significant difference between the modulated and non-modulated UiO-66 solids is the slightly higher proportion of \(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)groups (288 eV) in the modulated samples, in agreement with previous characterization data on the incorporation of acetate groups within the SBU instead of terephthalate derivatives.
The optical properties of the modulated and non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids were analysed by UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (Figs. S34 and S35 in the ESM). Figure 5 shows the absorption features of the most photocatalytically active UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-12AA. In good agreement with previous reports, the presence of the electron donor \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group in the terephthalate
organic ligand favours the absorption of light up to about \(450\;\mathrm{nm}\) compared to the white UiO-66(Zr) solid [56]. Although to a lesser extent, the presence of \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) groups in the UiO-66(Zr) solid also favours a bathochromic shift of the absorption up to wavelengths of about \(400\;\mathrm{nm}\). The estimated optical band gap for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA and UiO-66(Zr)-12AA by using the Tauc plot method was 2.85 and \(3.10\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. A comparative analysis of modulated and non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) using AA or TFA revealed the negligible influence of these modulators on their estimated optical band gaps (Figs. S34 and S35 in the ESM), indicating the important role of terephthalate organic ligands in determining the optical properties of these solids.
XPS was further used to estimate the valence band of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\))-12AA solids and compare it with their pristine MOFs (Fig. S36 in the ESM). The results indicated a small but significant positive shift of the highest occupied crystal orbital (HOCO) values measured for modulated samples compared to the parent MOFs. In a closely related precedent, theoretical calculations revealed that, compared to the ideal 12-fold coordinated nodes, in the UiO-66(Zr) structure, the related structures with 10- or 9-fold coordinated linkers increase the charge transfer (CT) capabilities [51]. Photoinduced electrons are thus preferentially located on the lowest, coordinated zirconium atoms of defective UiO-66 solids, a situation that is expected to increase the photocatalytic activity of defective solids. Other study using an optimized and partially decarboxylated MIL-125(Ti)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solid showed that this material also exhibited a small shift towards positive valence values compared to the parent MOFs while having practically the same optical band gap [28]. These results show the possibility of fine-tuning the electronic properties of MOFs by missing-linker strategies. The details of the calculations to estimate the energy band level of MOF shown in Fig. 5 versus the NHE can be found in the ESM (section S1 in the ESM). Together with other characterization techniques, these energy level diagrams will be employed later to explain the observed order of photocatalytic activity [13, 63].
## 3.2 Photocatalytic activity
The aim of this study was to investigate the influence of carboxylate organic modulators such as AA or TFA on UiO-66 photocatalysts in water splitting reactions. AA and TFA are
common carboxylic acid modulators used to prepare UiO-66 solids with specific density of structural defects, physicochemical, textural, and electronic properties compared to the ideal parent MOF. The photocatalytic activities of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) with or without organic modulators were first evaluated for generating hydrogen from water and methanol mixtures under simulated sunlight irradiation. Pristine UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) exhibits relatively higher activity than the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) photocatalyst (see Fig. 6). This could be associated with UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)'s lower band gap, which provides a more efficient absorption of simulated solar light irradiation and favours the proton reduction reaction towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) formation.
Interestingly, the UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids synthesized with different equivalents of AA (12, 36, or 100 eq.) or TFA (12 or 36 eq.) as modulators are more active than the parent MOFs for HER under simulated sunlight irradiation (Figs. 6(a) and 6(b)). The most active UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids with respect to their parent MOFs are those prepared with 12 eq. of AA. In addition, Figs. 6(c) and 6(d) show the hydrogen production during the photocatalytic HER reaction versus the number of linker defects per \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) formula unit for UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) referred as \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6+x}(\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{6-x}\) or
\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6+x}(\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{6-x}\) These figures reveal a volcano-like relationship between the number of defects \((x)\) introduced by using AA or TFA as modulators respect to the parent samples with the photocatalytic activities. The UiO-66 solids prepared with 12 eq. of AA exhibit the highest photocatalytic activities and are gradually decreased for the samples prepared with 36 or 100 eq of AA. Similarly, UiO-66 samples prepared with 12 eq. of TFA exhibit higher activity compared to those solids prepared with 36 eq.
Initially, the possible influence of the BET surface area or MOF particle size on the resulting photocatalytic activity was evaluated. Modulated synthesis of UiO-66 solids prepared with AA (12, 36 or 100 eq.) or TFA (12 eq.) results in an increased BET surface areas respect to the parent samples (Table 1). However, the UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) samples prepared with 12 eq. of TFA do not correspond to the highest photocatalytic activity (Fig. 6). Additionally, UiO-66 solids prepared with 12, 36, or 100 eq of AA have almost similar BET values but exhibit different photocatalytic activities. Furthermore, UiO-66 solids prepared with 36 eq. of TFA exhibit a significant decrease in BET values compared to those prepared with 12 eq. of TFA. However, the differences in the activities are relatively lower. Therefore, the observed
photocatalytic activities for the different UiO-66 defective solids suggest that the BET surface area is not a factor that determines the photocatalytic activity.
Additionally, UiO-66 solids prepared with \(12\,\mathrm{eq.}\) of AA exhibit the smallest particle sizes of the series while maintaining high surface areas (Table 1) and this situation might favour their photocatalytic performances. However, some UiO-66 solids prepared with 36 or 100 eq. of AA have larger particle sizes than pristine solids but exhibiting higher activities compared to nonmodulated MOFs. Moreover, even though the particle size of UiO\(66(\mathrm{Zr})\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) -36AA is considerably higher \((344\pm177)\) than UiO\(66(\mathrm{Zr})\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) -12TFA \((175\pm63)\) , the photocatalytic activity of the former solid in the HER is almost similar. Thus, these results imply that besides particle size of UiO-66 solids, some other factors should also contribute to the observed photocatalytic activities. In fact, it is anticipated that the increased activity of the defective UiO-66 solids is related to the efficient photoinduced charge separation and lower charge transfer resistance as evidenced by transient photocurrent and EIS measurements, respectively (Section 3.3.1 mechanistic evidence). Further, DFT calculations (Section 3.3.2) are also performed with UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids prepared using AA or TFA as modulators and the observed data suggest that this is an appropriate strategy to
decrease the energy barriers during both HER or oxygen evolution reaction (OER) respect to non-modulated solids.
\(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) prepared with \(12\,\mathrm{AA}\) as the most active photocatalyst in the series was also found to be reusable without a significant loss of activity (Fig. 7(a)). PSRD (Fig. 7(b)) and inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) measurements of the liquid phase after the reaction showed that the three-times used solids retain their crystallinity with the absence of zirconium leaching, respectively. Additionally, ICP-AES analyses of acid digested used UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA solid reveal similar zirconium content compared to the fresh sample \((32.5\;\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) . In agreement with previous studies, the deposition of Pt NPs \((2.48\pm0.49\;\mathrm{nm})\) as noble metal reference co-catalyst (Fig. S29 in the ESM) for the photocatalytic HER within UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA solid further increases its photocatalytic activity to \(2263\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}\) .
The \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NO}_{2}\) materials prepared with \(12\,\mathrm{AA}\) eq. as modulator and their parent solids were further evaluated as photocatalysts for the OWS under simulated sunlight irradiation. For the sake of comparison, these experiments were performed under the same conditions used for photocatalytic HER but replacing MeOH by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . The photocatalytic activity for OWS of \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)-NH}_{2}\) -12AA is higher compared to non-
modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. 7(c)). Similarly, the photocatalytic activity of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-12AA is \(30\%\) higher (233 and \(89\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), respectively, after \(5\,\mathrm{h}\)) than pristine solid. The considerably lower activity of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-12AA compared to UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA associated with the higher band gap of the former. Importantly, photocatalytic OWS using UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA sample with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}{}^{18}\mathrm{O}\) and the subsequent analysis of the evolved gases by gas chromatographymass spectrometry (GC-MS) showed \(m/z\) of 36 which confirms the formation of \({}^{18}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Fig. S37 in the ESM). This control experiment clearly proves the photocatalytic OWS oxidation pathway mediated by UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA. The use of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA modified with Pt NPs (Figs. S42 and S43 in the ESM) further increases the photocatalytic activity compared to UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA for the OWS (Fig. 7(c)). In this case, ICP-AES analyses of the liquid phase after the OWS reaction show the presence of zirconium \((1.7\,\mathrm{mg/L})\) and platinum \((0.094\,\mathrm{mg/L})\) leaching that represent \(1\,\mathrm{wt.}\%\) and \(1.9\,\mathrm{wt.}\%\) respect to the initial amounts of these metals in the fresh catalyst, respectively. Furthermore, ICP-AES analyses of acid-digested used Pt NPs supported UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA reveal the above conclusions on the occurrence of Pt or Zr leaching.
Figure 7(c) and Fig. S38 in the ESM show that the ratio of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production between 2 and \(5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) is higher compared to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production during the photocatalytic OWS. Based on previous related studies [13, 26, 64, 65], one possible explanation for this experimental observation might be related with the in situ \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction reaction during the photocatalytic process leading to the formation of reactive oxygen species or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Thus, the possible formation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) during the photocatalytic OWS using UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA or UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-12AA was proved by titration of reaction aliquots at different reaction times with titanyl oxalate as previously reported [26, 66]. The obtained results do not show the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) during the OWS reaction. Therefore, the lower rate of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production compared to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) might be related
with the formation of non-measurable amounts of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) by using the titanyl oxalate method and/or the occurrence of other side reactions that consume photogenerated \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Other possible reasons such as the saturation of MOF porosity by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) that is more soluble than \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) may also be a reason of the different observed gas rates during the reaction [26].
XRD patterns of the used UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA sample show some changes compared to the fresh one after photocatalytic OWS (Fig. 7(d)). Additionally, XPS comparisons of the fresh and used UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA show no significant differences between the spectra (Figs. S39 and S40 in the ESM). Furthermore, SEM or SEM-EDX (Fig. S41 in the ESM) characterization of the used UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA do not show significant changes of particle size distribution or elemental distributions. In contrast, analysis of the gas phase after the photocatalytic OWS reaction reveals the presence of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((87\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}\) after \(5\,\mathrm{h})\). In this context, previous studies have shown that carboxylate-based MOFs can undergo partial decarboxylation under UV–vis irradiation [14]. Therefore, it is proposed that UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA might undergo some partial decarboxylation during the reaction.
The photocatalytic activity of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA was compared with that achieved in previous studies using related solids and the results are summarised in Table S3 in the ESM. As it can be seen in this table, UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA is more active in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production of 450 and \(160\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively, after \(5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) than to previously reported MOF-based catalysts under simulated or visible light irradiation (Table S1 in the ESM), ca \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) productions in \(22\,\mathrm{~h~}\) below 400 and \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively, including UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2(\mathrm{core})@UiO-66(Ce)}(\mathrm{shell})\) [26], UiO-66(Ce)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with Pt NPs \((1\,\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) as co-catalyst [64], UiO-66(Zr/Ce/Ti) [67], MIL-125(Ti)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) treated with oxygen-plasma [28, 68], Ti-MOF (IEF-11) [69] and comparable or higher to that of porphyrin-based MOFs like MIL-173(Zr/Ti)-40 \((40\,\mathrm{wt.}\%\) of Ti content) [70], PCN-222(Zn) [65] or liposome-MOF built from Pt-porphyrin, \([\mathrm{Ru}(2,2\mathrm{bipyridine})_{3}]^{2+}\) and Ir
bipyridine catalytic centers [71]. Besides, UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA achieved estimated apparent quantum yield (AQY) of 0.13, 0.06 and \(0.04\,\%\) upon irradiations at 350, 400 and \(450\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. These AQY values are higher compared to a MOF-on-MOF composite built of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2(\mathrm{core})}@\mathrm{UiO-66}(\mathrm{Ce})_{\mathrm{(shell)}}\) with the value of \(0.034\%\) at \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) [26]. Not surprisingly, a couple of precedents using porphyrin-based MOFs such as MIL-173(Zr/Ti)-40 [70] (AQY at \(450\ \mathrm{nm}\) of \(0.11\%\) ) or liposome-based MOF [71] containing Pt metalated porphyrin units together with \([\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\) bipyridine) \(_3\)]\(^{2+}\) and Ir-bipyridine catalytic centers (AQY at \(436\;\mathrm{nm}\) of \(1.5\%\,\pm\,1\%\) at \(436\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) reported somehow higher AQY than UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA. In the present case, photodeposition of Pt NPs as the benchmark HER co-catalyst in UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA (Figs. S42 and S43 in the ESM) solid further increases the resulting activity for the photocatalytic OWS (Fig. 7(c)). Additionally, the estimated solar-to-hydrogen (STH) achieved with UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA sample during the photocatalytic OWS reaction is \(0.022\%\). This STH value is higher compared to Ni-based phosphonate MOF \((0.0001\%)\) as photocatalyst [72]. Overall, these results indicate that the preparation of defective UiO-66 solids by modulated synthesis is a convenient strategy to boost the photocatalytic activity of the samples towards HER and the more challenging OWS under simulated sunlight irradiation.
## 3.3 Reaction mechanism
## 3.3.1 Spectroscopic and electrochemical study
Several spectroscopic and electrochemical analysis were carried out to analyse the observed photocatalytic activity using the series of modulated UiO-66 samples. Figure 8(a) shows that the modulated synthesis of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) using either AA or TFA has an effect in their fluorescence properties, displaying much lower intensity than the parent solid. In this regard, the most photocatalytically active UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA displayed the lowest fluorescence of all derivatives. Moreover, in good agreement with these observations, the presence of Pt NPs \((1\ \mathrm{wt.}\%)\) in UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA enhanced the photocatalytic activity for both HER and OWS and strongly reduced fluorescence intensity. Hence, the fluorescence results can thus be associated with the improvement of photoinduced charge separation in modulated samples with respect to pristine UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Similarly, lower fluorescence emission when using Pt NPs supported UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA further supports the dual role of the co-catalyst in favouring both photoinduced charge separation and promoting the proton reduction reaction [13]. Similar fluorescence measurements were carried out using UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) solids (Fig. 8(b)). In this case, the lower emission of the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) samples than UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) samples is in
line with their corresponding organic ligand emission in solution (Fig. 8(b) inset). Further characterization of UiO-66 samples prepared with and without modulators by time-resolved fluorescence spectroscopy to get more information about electron–hole pair recombination indicates that these samples exhibit lifetimes below the quantification limit of our instrument \((<2\ \mathrm{ns})\) (Fig. S44 in the ESM). Overall, it can also be concluded that the preparation of modulated UiO-66 solids resulted in a concomitant relative fluorescence quenching that agrees, to some extent, with the volcano trend relationship between photocatalytic activity and number of defects shown in Fig. 6.
To study the photoinduced charge separation efficiency of the UiO-66 samples in depth, they were analysed by (photo)electrochemical techniques, and the results were compared with their corresponding non-modulated solids. Figures 9(a) and 9(b) show the photocurrent results obtained using a working electrode of modulated and non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. 9(a)) or UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Fig. 9(b)) solids supported on a transparent fluorine-doped tin oxide (TTO) electrode submerged in an acetonitrile solution \((0.1\ \mathrm{M},\ \mathrm{NB}_{44}\mathrm{PF}_{6})\) and submitted to a polarization voltage of \(+0.9\ \mathrm{V}\) and several on/off cycles of simulated sunlight irradiation. As can be seen in these figures, the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids had much higher photocurrent intensities than the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) solids, possibly due to UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)'s better ability to absorb simulated sunlight than UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), as shown by UV–vis DRS (Figs. S34 and S35 in the ESM). Previous studies found that the highest photocurrent intensities achieved with UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids were due to their better charge carrier separation, since the amino group can stabilize the photogenerated holes [73]. Importantly, Figs. 9(a) and 9(b) show that modulated UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids synthesized with AA (12, 36 or 100 eq.) or TFA (12 or 36 eq.) have enhanced photocurrent intensities respect to non-modulated MOFs. The highest photocurrent intensities achieved by UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) solids prepared using 12 eq. of AA and gradually decreased for analogous samples prepared with 36 or 100 eq. of AA. The samples prepared with 12 or 36 eq. of TFA show similar photocurrent responses. It is interesting to note that the order of photocurrent intensity agrees, to some extent, with the observed order of photocatalytic activity as a function of the number of defects (Fig. 6). The highest photoinduced charge separation efficiency of modulated samples with 12 eq. of AA results in the photocatalysts with the highest activities. It should be commented that the current intensity is due to the number of electrons extracted from the material and this measurement gives a quantification of the number of active sites in each solid [74].
Figures 9(c) and 9(d) show the photocurrent results obtained with a working electrode of modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA
(Fig. 9(c)) or \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (Fig. 9(d)) solids and parent MOFs supported on a transparent FTO electrode submerged in an acetonitrile solution \((0.1\;\mathrm{M},\;\mathrm{NBu}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6})\), submitted to different polarization voltages (from 1.7 to \(-0.2\;\mathrm{V}\)) with several on/off cycles under simulated sunlight irradiation. These results indicate again that modulated UiO-66 solids have the highest photoinduced charge separation efficiencies, thus exhibiting higher photocatalytic activity. The influence of methanol on the photocurrent measurements was studied to mimic the conditions during photocatalytic HER with water/methanol mixtures as the reaction medium for the most active \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}12\mathrm{AA}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}{-}12\mathrm{AA}\) samples. Figures 9(a) and 9(b) show that methanol with the series of UiO-66 solids in all cases raises the photocurrent intensities higher than in experiments with acetonitrile as the solvent. The modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) samples again show a better photocurrent response than pristine MOFs. Firstly, the better photocurrent intensity in the presence of methanol is in line with its role as sacrificial electron donor and partly reduces the electron/hole recombination. This facilitates the flow of electrons through the electrochemical cell and increases the photocurrent response, in agreement with greater photocatalytic hydrogen production using 12AA modulated and pristine \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids in the presence of MeOH than in OWS experiments. Secondly, the highest photocurrent response of modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) samples in the presence of methanol again reinforces their higher photoinduced charge carrier efficiencies, in accordance with their photocatalytic activities during HER. The comparison of Figs. 9(a) and 9(b) shows that the influence of methanol on the resulting photocurrent responses of modulated and nonmodulated samples is higher in the case of the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) solid. This is in good agreement with the relatively higher photocatalytic HER enhancement when using modulated and nonmodulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) than with the corresponding \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) ones.
EIS was used to evaluate the charge transfer resistance of
modulated and non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids (Fig. 10 and Table S4 in the ESM). Figures 10(a) and 10(b) show the Nyquist plots of the series of samples supported in a FTO electrode. An initial comparison shows that non-modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) has a smaller Nyquist plot arc radius than UiO\(66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) with charge transfer resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\) values of 3540 and 3775 \(\Omega_{\mathrm{r}}\) respectively. The modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids also exhibit a smaller arc radius than pristine UiO66 ones, indicating a lower charge transfer resistance of modulated UiO-66 solids versus non-modulated solids as well as \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) versus \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) (see Table S4 in the ESM). Interestingly, the Nyquist plot arc radius of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids prepared with AA or TFA as modulators follows the increasing order \(12\ <\ 36\ <\ 100\) eq. and \(12\ <\ 36\) eq., respectively. Furthermore, analogous EIS measurements performed using pristine \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids or analogous prepared with 12 eq. of AA in sunlight, which promote photoinduced charge carrier transfer, further reduce the charge resistance with respect to dark conditions (Figs. 10(c) and 10(d) and Table S4 in the ESM).
In brief, the EIS and photocurrent results indicate that the higher photocurrent intensity and lower charge transfer resistance of the modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) solids is in line with their enhanced photocatalytic activity and volcano trend relationship with the number of defects (Fig. 6).
Modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{X}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids with 12AA and their parent MOFs were studied by TAS in the microsecond time scale using the LFP technique to investigate the photoinduced charge carrier dynamics in greater depth using the most active photocatalysts. Initially, Ar-purged acetonitrile suspensions of these solids were prepared with the same optical density (ca. 0.35) at the excitation wavelength of \(266\;\mathrm{nm}\). The TAS recorded for the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}12\mathrm{AA}\) sample had a continuous absorption band ranging from 300 to \(750\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 11(a)). To evaluate the nature of the transient absorption species of this spectrum, an analogous measurement was carried out in the presence of a hole
quencher such as methanol. Thus, excitation of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-12AA in the presence of methanol resulted in quenching of the transient absorption in the region of about 350 to \(560~\mathrm{nm}\), while the 560-to- \(750~\mathrm{nm}\) intensity signal was enhanced. Methanol acts as a sacrificial electron donor in such a way that is oxidized by photogenerated holes and partially avoids the electron/hole pair recombination. The TAS results indicate that the quenching and enhancement of the absorption signals in the presence of methanol can be attributed to the holes and electrons in these regions, respectively. Similar conclusions can be drawn when performing analogous LEP experiments using \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})\) - \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA (Fig. S45 in the ESM) or non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. S46 in the ESM) solids. The TAS analysis of modulated UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -12AA solid reveals that regions of holes and electron are located at about 300–500 and \(550{-}700\ \ \mathrm{nm},\) respectively (Fig. 11(b)). Similar features were observed in the case of non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2},\) except for two electron regions at 500–560 and \(650{-}750\,\mathrm{nm}\).
A comparative study of the kinetics of the samples at different wavelengths was performed to investigate the photochemical behaviour. Figures 11(c) and 11(d) show the transient absorption decay traces at \(460\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the UiO-66 series after excitation at \(266~\mathrm{nm}\). Quantitative information on these decays was obtained from trace adjustment by a mono- or bi-exponential law according to Eq. S1 in the ESM and the results summarised in Table 2. Based on previous studies on MOF-based photocatalysts, the faster and more intense trace component can be associated with photoinduced charge separation, while the longer-lived and less intense second component is more closely related to charge delocalization [75]. Figure 11(c) shows that modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA displays faster relaxation kinetics than pristine UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Deposition of Pt NPs in UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA accelerates slightly but significantly this process. In this context, related studies have proposed that the presence of Pt NPs in a photocatalyst can favour additional electron transfer channels that results in accelerating the kinetics [75]. Accordingly, this was observed for \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}12\mathrm{AA},\) and it is associated with the opening of a new channel mediated by the noble NPs. Interestingly, the fastest relaxation kinetics achieved with Pt@UiO
\(66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA vs. UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA or UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA vs. UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) correlates with their highest photocatalytic activities. In contrast, the lowest relaxation kinetics achieved, for example non-modulated UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)), are related to trapping the photoinduced charge carriers in the MOF, which can hardly be used during the reactions [75]. In the case of modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -12AA compared to pristine MOF, the faster the relaxation kinetics also agrees with the higher photocatalytic activity. Similar conclusions can be drawn for the series of UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) after excitation at \(266~\mathrm{nm}\) and recording the trace kinetics at \(680~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figs. S47 and S48 and Table S5 in the ESM).
Previous studies using ESR spectroscopy have demonstrated that irradiation of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) based solids results in a photoinduced charge separation from the organic ligand to the metal node [76, 77]. This process results in the transformation of \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\) into \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{III})\) species that has been proposed as reductive active sites [76, 78]. To get some information about the reaction mechanism that takes place with UiO-66(Zr)-X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -12A) solids, solid-state ESR measurements have been carried out. Initially, ESR spectra of UiO-66 solids were recorded under dark reaction conditions. Figure 12(a) and Fig. S49(a) in the ESM show that pristine and modulated UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids show the presence of ESR signals that have been previously ascribed to the presence of \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{III})\) sites [76, 77, 79, 80]. In contrast, analogous measurements using UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) based solids (Fig. 12(b) and Fig. S49(b) in the ESM) do not show the presence of any paramagnetic signal. Importantly, irradiation of UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -12AA or UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) -12AA results in the formation of a new ESR signal at \(g=2.004\) that can be associated with the generation of \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{III})\) species via photoinduced electron transfer processes [76, 77, 79]. Regardless of the more intense signal intensities observed with UV–vis, irradiations with simulated sunlight also allow the formation of these species but with less intensities. These ESR results agree with previous characterizations like fluorescence, TAS, electrochemical measurements by EIS and photocurrent measurements, thus indicating the formation of \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{III})\) during the photocatalytic process.
## 3.3.2 DFT calculations
To shed light on the HER and OER mechanisms in the defective UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA, and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA, we systematically investigated the thermodynamics of these processes for the three MOFs by means of DFT calculations. As a first stage, periodic defective MOF structure models encompassing one defective linker per \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) formula unit saturated by the terminal functions explored experimentally in the three scenarios were constructed as illustrated in Fig. S2 in the ESM. Herein, the exposed \(\mathrm{Zr}\) site is assumed to be the catalytic site of the reaction and the adjacent \(\mathrm{Zr}\) site is graded by AA and TFA in a monodentate fashion. Such assumption has been widely established and used in studies of defective MOF catalysis [81–84]. Gibbs free energy \((\Delta G)\) curves corresponding to the HER and OER intermediates were then calculated for each scenario. The corresponding data reported in Figs. 13(a) reveal that for HER reaction, UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA \((\Delta G=0.56\;\mathrm{eV})\) and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA \((\Delta G=0.62\;\mathrm{eV})\) are associated with similar free energies that are much lower compared to the value obtained for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((\Delta G=2.47\;\mathrm{eV})\). This emphasizes that the modulation of the terminal functions makes UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) more effective for HER, UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA being predicted to be the best candidate in line with the experimental trend shown in Fig. 6(a). In the meantime, the calculated free energy for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is positive, indicating that the adsorption of the H species towards
the \(\mathrm{Zr}\) exposed sites in UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is weaker than that in the other two defective forms. This is corroborated by the analysis of the DFT-optimized distance between the H and \(\mathrm{Zr}\) sites, which demonstrated that the \(\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{Zr}\) distance in UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is the longest one (2.01 and \(1.93\;\mathrm{\AA}\), for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA). Moving now to the OER process, Fig. 13(b) illustrates for each elementary step the Gibbs free energy calculated for the four-electron reaction pathway as depicted in Fig. 13(c). For UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA, \([\mathrm{OH}]^{+} \rightarrow \mathrm{O}^{+} + (\mathrm{H}^{+} + \mathrm{e}^{-})\) was determined to be the rate-determining step (RDS), while \([\mathrm{OOH}]^{+} \rightarrow \mathrm{O}_{2} + \mathrm{O}_{3} + (\mathrm{H}^{+} + \mathrm{e}^{-})\) was found as RDS for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA (Fig. S50 in the ESM). The corresponding \([\Delta G]_{\mathrm{maxOER}}\) are 2.69, 2.46, and \(2.45\;\mathrm{eV}\) for UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA, and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA, respectively. Therefore, the computed thermodynamic overpotential of the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA and UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-TFA (1.23 and \(1.22\;\mathrm{V}\)) is clearly lower than the value obtained for the UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (1.46 V). As a result, our calculations confirmed the experimental observations that UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-AA outperform UiO-66(Zr)-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figs. 6 and 7) and showed that the improved catalytic activity of the exposed \(\mathrm{Zr}\) site with AA functionalization MOF is responsible for their good performance.
In a subsequent step, with the aim to gain an in-depth understanding of the underlying mechanisms for the intermediate's adsorption on the different UiO-66-NH2 forms, we further
analyzed their electronic structure properties. In this context, d-band center of catalyst near to the Fermi level should yield in principle to strong adsorption, whereas d-band center offsets which are too negative/positive hinder the adsorption process. As shown in Fig. 13(d), UiO-66(Zr)-NH2, UiO-66(Zr)-NH2-AA, and UiO-66(Zr)-NH2-TFA exhibit d-band center offsets amounting to 2.93, 1.37, and \(1.36\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively due to the energy stabilization induced by the AA and TFA functional groups. On the same vein, according to the Bader charge population analysis and the charge density difference profiles (Fig. S51 in the ESM), a more pronounced electron transfer from the Zr active site to \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)occurs for UiO-66(Zr)-NH2-AA \((0.53\,\mathrm{e}^{-})\) when compared to UiO-66(Zr)-NH2 \((0.42\,\mathrm{e}^{-})\). This indicates that the charge transfer from Zr to the \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)intermediate enhances the adsorption of \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)on UiO-66(Zr)-NH2-AA, and results in an enhanced HER activity. Notably, compared to the structure models used for the exploration of the catalytic reaction with \(1\,\mathrm{Zr}\) exposed site and 1 neighbouring Zr site saturated by AA and TFA, here when focusing only on the modification of the opto-electronic properties due to the presence of the modulators, this spatial constraint is not anymore required and both oxygen atoms from the carboxylic anchoring groups can be coordinated to the Zr sites. In this viewpoint, with the aim of
for UiO-66(Zr)-NH2-AA \((0.53\,\mathrm{e}^{-})\) when compared to UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((0.42\;\mathrm{e}^{-})\) . This indicates that the charge transfer from \(Z\mathbf{r}\) to the \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\) intermediate enhances the adsorption of \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\) on UiO-66(Zr)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -AA, and results in an enhanced HER activity. Notably, compared to the structure models used for the exploration of the catalytic reaction with \(1\,\mathrm{Zr}\) exposed site and 1 neighbouring \(Z\mathbf{r}\) site saturated by AA and TFA, here when focusing only on the modification of the opto-electronic properties due to the presence of the modulators, this spatial constraint is not anymore required and both oxygen atoms from the carboxylic anchoring groups can be coordinated to the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) sites. In this viewpoint, with the aim of
rationalizing the impact of the modulators (AA and TFA) in the optical response of the defective materials, we have investigated the excited state properties of the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) cluster models bearing AA and TFA moieties coordinated in a bidentate manner on the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) sites by means of TD-DFT calculations (see for more details in the ESM). The absorption spectra of the pristine defective (OH) and the AA and TFA coordinated clusters are displayed in Fig. 14(a). Notably, our simulated spectra nicely reproduced the absorption features from the UV–vis diffuse reflectance plots represented in Fig. 5 and Fig. S34 in the ESM, bearing three main bands in these energy regions: one in the near vis centered at \(370\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the two others in the UV peaked at 265 and \(225\,\mathrm{nm}\) (see Table S5 in the ESM). Regarding the AA and TFA spectra, only a slight increase in the intensity of the lowest energy absorption bands was observed when compared to the reference OH system, which is fully consistent with the similar absorption characteristics measured for all set of samples analyzed experimentally (Fig. S34 in the ESM). Furthermore, identical trends followed by the absorption features of the AA/TFA vs. OH systems were obtained for the simulated spectra of the periodic materials, as they are represented in Fig. S52 in the ESM (see also Table S6 in the ESM for the properties of the main excited states of the spectra). In this viewpoint, in order to get a deeper understanding on the nature of these absorption bands, we carried out two types of excitation analysis: the natural transition orbital (NTO) [84] plots which provided us a spatial representation of the distribution of the photogenerated charges (holes and electrons), and the charge transfer analysis which allowed us to get a quantitative estimation of the delocalization of both particles along the ligands and the metallic nodes, as well as the extent of the CT induced by this excitation. Interestingly, the NTOs plotted in Fig. 14(b) and Fig. S53 in the ESM demonstrated that both holes and electrons are mostly localized on the \(\mathrm{BDC}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) ligands, being holes especially confined in one of the BDC moieties, while electrons remained localized on both ligands with a certain extent of delocalization around the \(Z\mathbf{rO}\) metal nodes. Indeed, a similar
electronic density delocalization was found for the energy levels involved in the main transitions of the simulated spectra of the periodic systems, as it can be observed in the frontier crystalline orbitals (COs) shape plots depicted in Fig. S54 in the ESM. On the same footing, the CT analysis revealed that the hole delocalization along the ligands amounted to \(90\%{-}100\%\) , whereas for electrons the extent of delocalization around the \(Z\mathbf{rO}\) metal cluster was equal to \(10\%{-}40\%\) . As a result, these states acquire a given ligand-to-metal CT (LMCT) character which amounted to \(\sim0.1\) for the vis region band, and \(\sim0.3–0.4\) for the UV bands. Interestingly, the observation of such LMCT processes in defective UiO-66 materials is congruent with previous theoretical works [50], and it evidences the role played by \(Z\mathbf{r}\) metal nodes as reductive active sites, as it has been confirmed by the transformation of \(Z\mathbf{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) into \(Z\mathbf{r}(\mathrm{III})\) measured via spectroscopic measurements [76, 77, 79]. The spatial distribution of the generated particles was indeed found independent of the terminal functions coordinated to the metal node, which goes in line with the fact that the most important absorption features of these MOFs arise from the ligand states. To sum up, we can conclude that light harvesting capacity of these porous materials is weakly affected by the modulator employed to prepare the materials, which clearly corroborates that the OWS activity enhancement induced by the presence of AA and TFA coordinated groups is mainly attributed to the lowering of the energy barriers for both HER and OER intermediates simulated above by DFT calculations.
Figure 15 summarizes the proposed photocatalytic mechanism during HER or OWS. Briefly, simulated sunlight irradiation of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}12\mathrm{AA}\) promotes the photoinduced electron transfer from the organic ligand to the metal node. Then, a one-electron reduction process results in the transformation of \(Z\mathbf{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) species into \(Z\mathbf{r}(\mathrm{III})\) ones that act as reductive active sites. It is proposed that the holes are stabilized in the organic ligand and are responsible for the oxidation of MeOH or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to the oxidized products or \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) during the HER or OWS, respectively.
## 4 Conclusions
This study has shown the influence of the synthesis of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\) X (X: \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) ) solids with common AA or TFA organic modulators on their resulting photocatalytic activity for the HER and OWS under simulated sunlight irradiation. The \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solids showed higher catalytic activity than \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2},\) associated with the lower band gap values and more negative lowest unoccupied crystal orbital (LUCO) values of the former solids than their analogous \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) materials. The UiO-66 solids modulated by either AA or TFA prepared in this study showed greater photocatalytic activity for HER than their corresponding pristine materials. The most active samples of each series were prepared using 12 AA eq. The most active \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) modulated sample also enhanced OWS photoactivity with respect to pristine \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) . The characterization of modulated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) by means of photocurrent and EIS revealed that these samples had a better charge transfer ability than their pattern solids. Additional LFP and PL measurements confirmed that the optimized modulated samples improved \(\mathrm{e^{-}/h^{+}}\) charge separation with respect to nonmodulated samples. Furthermore, theoretical calculations revealed that, whereas the presence of modulators barely modified the absorption features of and the extent of LMCT towards the metal node in the defective UiO-66 materials, they had a major impact in the thermodynamics of the OWS reactions via a significant lowering of energy barriers for reaction for both HER and OER processes. Importantly, the optimized \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) prepared
with 12 eq. of AA modified or not with Pt NPs (1 wt.%) as cocatalyst exhibited better photocatalytic activity than analogous MOF-based materials for OWS under simulated sunlight irradiation.
The authors believe that this study will contribute to the development of defective MOF-based photocatalysts for solar-driven OWS process.
